## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are: the flu vaccination event was officially documented by NHS England as part of a public health campaign; standard medical procedure dictates that a needle with a cap attached would never be used for an injection; and the described equipment is likely a prefilled syringe with an integrated needle guard safety mechanism, which could be visually mistaken for a cap. The evidence provides no support for the claim of faking and instead aligns with a legitimate medical procedure.

No further information is required to reach a decision, as the evidence clearly addresses the core elements of the claim regarding the procedure and equipment.

The claim that Sadiq Khan faked his flu jab due to the plastic cap being left on is directly contradicted by the official record of a real vaccination event and the explanation of standard medical equipment that includes safety features, not disposable caps. Therefore, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted based on evidence showing that the vaccination event was officially documented by [NHS England](https://www.england.nhs.uk/london/2016/11/10/mayor-of-london-flu-jab/) as part of the "Stay Well This Winter" campaign. The object mistaken for a plastic cap is consistent with the [integrated needle guard safety mechanism](https://www.mskcc.org/cancer-care/patient-education/how-give-yourself-subcutaneous-injection-using-prefilled-syringe) found on modern prefilled syringes, and standard medical procedures explicitly [prohibit the use of a needle with a cap attached](https://www.tn.gov/content/dam/tn/health/documents/immunizationrequirements/vfc/KnowTheSite-VaccineAdministration.pdf).